         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "a7b8c9d0-e1f2-4a3b-4c5d-6e7f8091a2b3",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [
            {
               "type": 1,
               "uuid": "7cc05340-732f-4c3d-b260-5437670fbc99",
               "text": "Original Text",
               "page": "https://www.historytracers.org/index.html?page=class_content&arg=7cc05340-732f-4c3d-b260-5437670fbc99",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2026",
                  "month": "",
                  "day": ""
               }
            },
            {
               "type": 0,
               "uuid": "a1a7c478-9061-4160-ba43-301c081733e9",
               "text": "ESA",
               "page": "https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Space_Science/Planck/History_of_cosmic_structure_formation",
               "date_time": {
                  "type": "gregory",
                  "year": "2025",
                  "month": "09",
                  "day": "17"
               }
            }
         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we learned that recent measurements have shown us the **universe is growing** -- expanding in every direction.\n\nAs a consequence, in the very distant past, everything we see today was **contracted into an extremely small space**. About 13.82 billion years ago, the universe began expanding, creating everything we observe.\n\nIn the next class, we are going to continue studying how everything could be confined in a small space and, billions of years later, become us and everything we see around us.",
